1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:01,357
A pea pod.

2
00:00:01,357 --> 00:00:02,257
An onion.

3
00:00:02,156 --> 00:00:03,316
A bowl of dal.

4
00:00:03,716 --> 00:00:06,445
Dal, peas and groundnuts all
come from one plant family.

5
00:00:06,445 --> 00:00:08,534
Onions, aloe and tulips come from another.

6
00:00:08,984 --> 00:00:10,794
Diya was helping in the kitchen.

7
00:00:10,794 --> 00:00:14,651
She wants to be a doctor, and she
asked: what makes a pea plant and an

8
00:00:14,651 --> 00:00:15,782
onion so different?

9
00:00:16,132 --> 00:00:18,102
One is a dicot family, Fabaceae.

10
00:00:18,102 --> 00:00:20,508
The other is a monocot family, Liliaceae.

11
00:00:20,508 --> 00:00:22,214
Their flowers tell the story.

12
00:00:22,714 --> 00:00:26,060
By the end, you will describe both
families, read their floral formulas,

13
00:00:26,060 --> 00:00:27,572
and spot them in your kitchen.

14
00:00:28,222 --> 00:00:29,122
First, a note.

15
00:00:29,083 --> 00:00:32,366
These two families are not in the
current NCERT text, but the ISC syllabus

16
00:00:32,366 --> 00:00:33,332
still teaches them.

17
00:00:33,682 --> 00:00:36,969
Fabaceae was earlier called
Papilionoideae, a part of a bigger family,

18
00:00:36,969 --> 00:00:37,869
Leguminosae.

19
00:00:37,647 --> 00:00:39,145
It grows all over the world.

20
00:00:39,595 --> 00:00:42,271
Its plants are trees, shrubs and herbs.

21
00:00:42,271 --> 00:00:45,597
Their roots carry small
lumps, called root nodules.

22
00:00:46,047 --> 00:00:50,303
The leaves are alternate, pinnately
compound or simple, with a swollen

23
00:00:50,303 --> 00:00:54,964
leaf base called the pulvinus, small
leafy stipules, and a net of veins.

24
00:00:55,614 --> 00:00:59,141
Let's zoom into a pea
flower, one part at a time.

25
00:00:59,491 --> 00:01:02,046
The flower is bisexual and zygomorphic.

26
00:01:02,046 --> 00:01:05,131
It splits into equal
halves in only one plane.

27
00:01:05,131 --> 00:01:07,028
Its five sepals are joined.

28
00:01:07,528 --> 00:01:10,724
It has five free petals,
shaped like a butterfly.

29
00:01:10,724 --> 00:01:15,223
One standard at the back, two wings
at the sides, and two in front that

30
00:01:15,223 --> 00:01:16,304
form the keel.

31
00:01:16,304 --> 00:01:18,209
This is vexillary aestivation.

32
00:01:18,709 --> 00:01:21,804
Inside the keel are ten
stamens, in two bundles.

33
00:01:21,804 --> 00:01:24,561
Nine are joined together, and one is free.

34
00:01:24,561 --> 00:01:26,292
This is called diadelphous.

35
00:01:26,792 --> 00:01:28,554
In the centre is one carpel.

36
00:01:28,554 --> 00:01:31,710
The ovary is superior, with
one room and many ovules.

37
00:01:31,710 --> 00:01:33,875
It becomes a pod, called a legume.

38
00:01:34,525 --> 00:01:37,740
Now the monocot family,
Liliaceae, the lily family.

39
00:01:37,740 --> 00:01:40,115
Let's build its flower, ring by ring.

40
00:01:40,465 --> 00:01:41,723
First, the plant.

41
00:01:41,723 --> 00:01:46,016
Perennial herbs that live on through
underground bulbs, corms or rhizomes.

42
00:01:46,016 --> 00:01:48,176
The leaves sit mostly at the base.

43
00:01:48,176 --> 00:01:51,029
They are long and narrow,
with parallel veins.

44
00:01:51,779 --> 00:01:52,679
Step one.

45
00:01:52,561 --> 00:01:55,099
The flower is bisexual and actinomorphic.

46
00:01:55,099 --> 00:01:58,533
Sepals and petals look alike,
so we call them tepals.

47
00:01:58,533 --> 00:02:02,712
There are six, in two rings of
three, often joined into a tube.

48
00:02:03,462 --> 00:02:04,362
Step two.

49
00:02:04,035 --> 00:02:06,512
Six stamens, three plus
three, fixed on the tepals.

50
00:02:06,512 --> 00:02:07,547
They are epitepalous.

51
00:02:08,297 --> 00:02:09,197
Step three.

52
00:02:09,088 --> 00:02:11,385
Three fused carpels in a superior ovary.

53
00:02:11,385 --> 00:02:14,705
It has three rooms, with many
ovules on the central axis.

54
00:02:14,705 --> 00:02:16,287
That is axile placentation.

55
00:02:17,037 --> 00:02:17,937
Step four.

56
00:02:17,838 --> 00:02:22,479
The fruit is a capsule, rarely a berry,
and the seeds keep their endosperm.

57
00:02:23,229 --> 00:02:24,383
Side by side.

58
00:02:24,383 --> 00:02:25,283
Symmetry.

59
00:02:25,195 --> 00:02:27,278
A pea flower is zygomorphic.

60
00:02:27,278 --> 00:02:29,522
A lily flower is actinomorphic.

61
00:02:30,272 --> 00:02:31,172
Petals.

62
00:02:30,779 --> 00:02:33,900
Fabaceae has five petals: a
standard, two wings and a keel.

63
00:02:33,900 --> 00:02:36,429
Liliaceae has six tepals,
in two rings of three.

64
00:02:37,179 --> 00:02:38,079
Stamens.

65
00:02:37,689 --> 00:02:39,868
Fabaceae has ten, nine
joined plus one free.

66
00:02:39,868 --> 00:02:41,823
Liliaceae has six, fixed on the tepals.

67
00:02:42,573 --> 00:02:43,473
Ovary.

68
00:02:43,031 --> 00:02:44,923
Fabaceae has one carpel and one room.

69
00:02:44,923 --> 00:02:47,348
Liliaceae has three fused
carpels and three rooms.

70
00:02:48,098 --> 00:02:48,998
Your turn.

71
00:02:48,860 --> 00:02:53,246
A flower has stamens in the pattern
nine plus one, and its fruit is a pod.

72
00:02:53,246 --> 00:02:54,146
Which family?

73
00:02:54,139 --> 00:02:55,259
Pause and decide.

74
00:02:58,459 --> 00:02:59,359
Fabaceae!

75
00:02:59,111 --> 00:03:02,290
Nine plus one is diadelphous,
and the pod is a legume.

76
00:03:02,940 --> 00:03:03,872
Remember this.

77
00:03:03,872 --> 00:03:06,372
Pea: five petals, nine plus one stamens.

78
00:03:06,372 --> 00:03:08,602
Lily: six tepals, three plus three.

79
00:03:09,202 --> 00:03:10,807
Now, a worked example.

80
00:03:10,807 --> 00:03:14,463
Sort six useful plants into
their families, one clue each.

81
00:03:14,813 --> 00:03:16,450
Gram gives us dal, a pulse.

82
00:03:16,450 --> 00:03:17,350
Fabaceae.

83
00:03:17,504 --> 00:03:18,663
Aloe gives a medicine.

84
00:03:18,663 --> 00:03:19,563
Liliaceae.

85
00:03:19,704 --> 00:03:21,261
Groundnut gives edible oil.

86
00:03:21,261 --> 00:03:22,161
Fabaceae.

87
00:03:22,355 --> 00:03:24,089
Tulip is grown for its flowers.

88
00:03:24,089 --> 00:03:24,989
Liliaceae.

89
00:03:25,189 --> 00:03:26,133
Indigofera gives a dye.

90
00:03:26,133 --> 00:03:27,033
Fabaceae.

91
00:03:27,021 --> 00:03:29,596
And Colchicum autumnale gives
the chemical colchicine.

92
00:03:29,596 --> 00:03:30,496
Liliaceae.

93
00:03:30,865 --> 00:03:32,327
Now, the exam trap.

94
00:03:32,677 --> 00:03:35,422
Many students write the stamens of
a pea flower as ten free stamens.

95
00:03:35,822 --> 00:03:39,474
But nine stamens are joined in
one bundle, and one is free.

96
00:03:39,474 --> 00:03:41,291
Nine in brackets, plus one.

97
00:03:41,291 --> 00:03:42,590
That is diadelphous.

98
00:03:43,240 --> 00:03:44,711
Where is this asked?

99
00:03:44,711 --> 00:03:49,273
In ISC papers, as describing a
family, or writing its floral formula.

100
00:03:49,873 --> 00:03:50,860
Exam level now.

101
00:03:50,860 --> 00:03:53,128
Which floral formula belongs to Liliaceae?

102
00:03:53,128 --> 00:03:54,157
Pause and solve.

103
00:03:59,557 --> 00:04:00,828
The answer is A.

104
00:04:01,328 --> 00:04:03,457
B is Fabaceae, with nine plus one stamens.

105
00:04:03,457 --> 00:04:05,506
C is Solanaceae, with five joined petals.

106
00:04:06,006 --> 00:04:09,892
Only A has six tepals in two rings
of three, and three fused carpels in

107
00:04:09,892 --> 00:04:10,974
a superior ovary.

108
00:04:11,574 --> 00:04:13,114
Here is your recap.

109
00:04:13,114 --> 00:04:14,697
Screenshot this card.

110
00:04:17,897 --> 00:04:20,082
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

111
00:04:20,482 --> 00:04:21,382
One.

112
00:04:20,935 --> 00:04:23,778
Name the three kinds of
petals in a pea flower.

113
00:04:24,678 --> 00:04:25,578
Two.

114
00:04:25,126 --> 00:04:26,743
What does diadelphous mean?

115
00:04:27,643 --> 00:04:28,543
Three.

116
00:04:27,949 --> 00:04:30,053
Give two differences between the
flowers of Fabaceae and Liliaceae.

117
00:04:31,153 --> 00:04:35,287
Next time: a lawn is cut short,
and it grows back from below.

118
00:04:35,287 --> 00:04:36,187
How?

119
00:04:35,780 --> 00:04:38,107
We find out in meristematic tissues.
